## Refactored with Async/Await

```javascript
async function getUser(id) {
  const rows = await db.query('SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ?', [id]);

  if (!rows.length) {
    throw new Error(`User with id "${id}" not found`);
  }

  return rows[0];
}
```

### Usage Example

```javascript
// ✅ Using try/catch for error handling
async function handleRequest(userId) {
  try {
    const user = await getUser(userId);
    console.log('Found user:', user);
  } catch (err) {
    console.error('Error:', err.message);
  }
}

// ✅ Using .catch() for error handling
getUser(userId)
  .then(user => console.log('Found user:', user))
  .catch(err => console.error('Error:', err.message));
```

---

### Key Changes

| Before | After |
|---|---|
| `callback(err)` | `throw new Error(...)` |
| `callback(null, rows[0])` | `return rows[0]` |
| Caller handles `err` param | Caller uses `try/catch` |
| Nested callbacks | Linear, readable flow |

---

### Important Caveat

This assumes `db.query` **already returns a Promise**. Many modern drivers (like `mysql2/promise`, `pg`, `knex`) do this natively.

If your driver only supports **callbacks**, wrap it first with `util.promisify`:

```javascript
const { promisify } = require('util');
const dbQuery = promisify(db.query).bind(db);

async function getUser(id) {
  const rows = await dbQuery('SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ?', [id]);

  if (!rows.length) {
    throw new Error(`User with id "${id}" not found`);
  }

  return rows[0];
}
```

> **Tip:** If `util.promisify` doesn't work cleanly (e.g., the callback signature is non-standard), you can manually wrap it in a `new Promise(...)` as a fallback.
